--TEST--
`Core\Cache\Store::put` is the graph copy's fourth carrier, so a `secret` value is refused there with the same code the other three report — and the help names `putSecret`, because this is the one carrier the language gives a secret a door of its own — `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`, `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary` refuses a `secret` at the one
// recursive graph copy, and `put` makes one: the entry it writes outlives the
// request and nothing on the far side of the copy carries the qualifier. The
// sealed door is the only door, and the diagnostic says which one it is rather
// than offering a reveal that would put a plaintext secret in a store.
class Vault {
    public static function token(): secret string {
        return "sk-live";
    }
}

string $plain = "plain";
tainted string $t = "t";
secret string $s = "s";

var $local = Core\Cache::local();
var $process = Core\Cache::process();

// The accepted side first, so this is a bound with both sides named. `tainted`
// is not on this sink's list: taint says who wrote the value, and an entry is
// no less entitled to it than the request that put it there.
$local->put("plain", $plain);
$local->put("tainted", $t);

// Written positionally.
$local->put("token", $s);

// Filled by name, which reaches the same parameter — the refusal is read off
// the argument's slot rather than off its position.
$local->put(key: "token", value: $s);

// Composed rather than declared, and on the other in-process tier: the
// qualifier poisons through concatenation, and a tier is a destination rather
// than a different operation, so both halves of that reach one refusal.
$process->put("token", "bearer " . $s);

// Declared one frame away, which is where a rule that only looked at variables
// would miss it.
$process->put("token", Vault::token());
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Cache\Store::put` copies it into a tier that outlives the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
   = help: write `putSecret($key, $value, $ttl, $keys)` instead, which seals the value under a key ring so that what reaches the tier is ciphertext; or reveal it explicitly first with `Core\Secret::reveal(..., "reason")`, at the one call site where handing the secret over is the point; a `secret`-typed *property* of a copied object needs nothing here — the walk refuses that one itself
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Cache\Store::put` copies it into a tier that outlives the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Cache\Store::put` copies it into a tier that outlives the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Cache\Store::put` copies it into a tier that outlives the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
%A
